-- RL-04 — the TWO-ORACLE parser differential.
--
-- `pass16_parser_corpus_proof.duo` pins what `lib/std/compiler/parser.duo`
-- projects. It is a ONE-oracle test: I wrote both the input and the expected
-- tree, so it can only catch the parser disagreeing with ME. It cannot catch
-- the parser disagreeing with `src/parser.zig`, which is the whole point of
-- RL-04 — the Duo parser is meant to REPLACE that file, and a replacement is
-- only defensible against the incumbent's own behaviour.
--
-- `scripts/parser_corpus_coverage.duo` has the mirrored blindness: it counts
-- files the Duo parser accepts, with no reference to whether the production
-- parser accepts them. 257/257 on lib/std reads as "done" and means "the Duo
-- parser accepts every file in the dialect lib/std happens to be written in".
--
-- ── THE SECOND ORACLE, with no `src/**` change ───────────────────────────────
--
-- `duo fmt` is `Parser.init(&lex).parse_module()` fed straight to
-- `PrettyPrinter.printModule` (src/main.zig `do_fmt`). Nothing else is in that
-- path — no sema, no codegen. So `duo fmt` IS `src/parser.zig`'s AST made
-- observable as text, and it already ships. Two readings come free:
--
--   ACCEPTANCE     `duo fmt` exits nonzero exactly when src/parser.zig rejects.
--   NORMALIZATION  its output is print(parse(S)) — the incumbent AST's own
--                  rendering of the same program.
--
-- ── THE THREE PROPOSITIONS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
--   P1 ACCEPTANCE AGREEMENT   zig accepts S  <=>  duo accepts S.
--        `duorej` is a HOLE: real Duo the self-hosted parser cannot read.
--        `zigrej` is the direction nobody had measured — the self-hosted parser
--        accepting source the production parser REJECTS. The coverage ratchet
--        is structurally blind to it, because over-acceptance moves the number
--        the good way.
--
--   P2 CANONICAL CLOSURE      zig accepts S  =>  duo accepts fmt(S).
--        A parser that cannot read its own toolchain's output cannot replace
--        that toolchain. No judgement call is in this one, which is why it is
--        the proposition that did the work below.
--
--   P3 PROJECTION STABILITY   proj(S) == proj(fmt(S)), unless the row says NORM.
--        fmt is AST-preserving by construction, so a projection that MOVES
--        under it is reading structure the incumbent AST does not have. The
--        NORM rows are where the incumbent legitimately desugars — or, twice
--        below, where it LOSES information — and both trees are pinned so the
--        normalization is checked by value rather than waved through.
--
-- ── WHAT IT FOUND ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Swept over the repo (lib/std 258 files, tools 45), before / after this
-- session's repairs:
--
--   P1  lib/std   258/258 agree, zero divergence, before and after. The
--                 257/257 ratchet is real and also says nothing: the
--                 production parser accepts all 258 too.
--       tools     9 -> 3 files the production parser accepts and this one
--                 rejects. The 9 were never 9 constructs; they were four:
--                   `then`-form `if`      6 files   CLOSED
--                   tuple return type     1 file    CLOSED
--                   string-literal desc key + pack-of-expressions (same file)
--                                                   CLOSED
--                   `match`/`case`        2 files   open, deny-listed
--                   list comprehension    1 file    open, deny-listed
--
--   P2  273 of 300 formatted files were UNREADABLE by the self-hosted parser
--       at session start — while P1 said 299/303 agreed. That gap is the
--       measurement the corpus ratchet cannot make. After the repairs: 27
--       remain, and 21 of those 27 are one `src/pretty.zig` defect (below).
--
-- gap[046] §3 filed `then` and `-> T` under "the parser is right and the source
-- is stale", because §1 deny-lists both. P2 overturns that, mechanically:
--
--     $ duo fmt --canonical hex.duo
--     encode(s: str) -> str        <- the arrow, in CANONICAL mode
--
--     if ready run() end   formats to   if ready then / run() / end
--
-- The production toolchain WRITES both. Deny-list status governs what a human
-- types; it has never governed what a parser must read, and conflating the two
-- is how a machine-generated file got recorded as stale source. `->` alone was
-- 224 of the P2 failures.
--
-- ── FINDINGS THAT ARE NOT MINE TO FIX (src/**) ──────────────────────────────
--
--   F1  CLOSED. `src/pretty.zig` printed the lens `.err` as
--       `|__proj_v| __proj_v.err` — a pipe-delimited closure that is not Duo
--       grammar at all, carrying a generated name that breaks LAW-ONE twice
--       (`_` prefix, `[a-z]_[a-z]`). Measured at 21 of 260 lib/std files
--       before, 0 after. The printer now re-sugars the verified desugaring
--       back to the anchor it came from, and every other lambda keeps its
--       `fun`, so no `|params|` is emitted anywhere.
--   F2  CLOSED. `duo fmt` WAS NOT IDEMPOTENT: `f = fun(a) a end` formatted to
--       `f = (a) / a / end`, and formatting THAT failed with "expected
--       '<eof>', got 'end'" — the incumbent could not re-read its own output
--       either, so row 35 is bothrej rather than a hole in this parser. A
--       lambda now keeps its `fun`, so its output reparses to an IDENTICAL
--       projection (row 43). Row 35 stays exactly as it is: its source is the
--       old broken rendering, which both parsers still reject and which the
--       formatter no longer produces.
--   F3  The incumbent DROPS the result union. `f(x: i64): i64 | error` formats
--       to `fun f(x: i64) -> i64` — `| error` is gone. Pass 100 §0.6 makes
--       that union the language's result discipline, and the self-hosted
--       parser now retains it where `src/parser.zig` does not. This is the
--       first construct where the replacement is AHEAD of the incumbent, and
--       it is why rows 20-21 are NORM rather than a defect here.
--   F4  The incumbent silently REPAIRS a truncated `do`: `do x = 1` parses
--       clean and formats to `do / x = 1 / end`. The self-hosted parser
--       rejects it, which is the stricter and correct side; the corpus proof
--       has always demanded that rejection.
--
-- Exit code: 0 all propositions hold. 1..99 P1 row, 100+ P2 row, 200+ P3 row,
-- 250 the oracle itself failed a positive control.

tmpdir = (): str
    std.script.getenv("DUO_TWO_ORACLE_TMP", "/tmp/duo-two-oracle")
end

probe = (): str
    tmpdir() .. "/probe.duo"
end

duobin = (): str
    std.script.getenv("DUO_ROOT", ".") .. "/zig-out/bin/duo"
end

-- Oracle A: src/parser.zig. Writes S to a scratch path, runs `duo fmt`, returns
-- its exit status. `duo fmt` rewrites in place, which is why the probe is a
-- COPY and never a repo path.
zig_accepts = (src: str): i64
    std.script.fs_write(probe(), src)
    std.script.status(duobin() .. " fmt " .. probe())
end

-- The incumbent AST's own rendering. Meaningful only after zig_accepts gave 0.
zig_render = (): str
    std.script.fs_read(probe())
end

-- Oracle B: lib/std/compiler/parser.duo.
duo_accepts = (src: str): i64
    std.compiler.parser.parse_status(src, "probe.duo")
end

-- Each row is (source, zig, duo, p2, stability).
--   zig / duo   0 = accepts, 1 = rejects
--   p2          0 = the Duo parser reads fmt(S), 1 = it does not (a pinned
--               finding, always with its cause named in the comment)
--   stability   "same" proj(S) == proj(fmt(S)) · "norm" they differ and the
--               reason is named · "-" not applicable, one oracle rejected
rows = {
    -- ── AGREEMENT, the boring majority ──────────────────────────────────────
    -- These are the harness's positive control. If `duo fmt` ever stops being a
    -- parser oracle — a formatter rewrite, a flag change, a missing binary —
    -- these go red first and loudly, instead of every row quietly reading
    -- "accepts, output unchanged".
    { "x = 42", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "add(a, b)", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "2 + 3 * (4 - 1)", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "while n > 0 n = n - 1 end", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "d = { x = 1, y = 2 }", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "for i = 1, n x = i end", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "x = t[i].f[2]", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "obj:m(1, 2)", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "x = a ^ b ^ c", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "n: i64 = 5", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "x = store(i32)(v)", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "t = { [0] = \"a\", [1] = \"b\" }", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "x = ~a", 0, 0, 0, "same" },

    -- ── `then`: the row P2 convicted ────────────────────────────────────────
    -- Before the repair the first of these was (0, 1) and proj(fmt(S)) was
    -- `(error)`: the formatter's own rendering of an `if` was unreadable. All
    -- four spellings are pinned because the arms differ — the head, an
    -- `elseif` head, and the expression-position `if` share proj_if but reach
    -- it by different paths.
    { "if ready run() end", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "if a then x = 1 end", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "if a then x = 1 else y = 2 end", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "if a then x = 1 elseif b then y = 2 else z = 3 end", 0, 0, 0, "same" },

    -- ── `-> T`: the row P2 convicted at scale ───────────────────────────────
    -- 224 of the 273 P2 failures. Both spellings project `(ret T)` with no
    -- record of which was written, because they name the same edge — if they
    -- projected differently, the formatter's output would be a different tree
    -- from its input by construction and P3 could never hold.
    { "p(b: any) -> bool\n  b\nend\n", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "fun p(b: any) -> bool\n  b\nend\n", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    -- A `concept` member re-emitted with BOTH the `fun` keyword and the arrow.
    -- lib/std/mem.duo writes the bare form and the formatter writes this one.
    { "concept A\n  fun alloc(s: any, n: i64) -> any\nend\n", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "concept A\n  alloc(s: any, n: i64): any\nend\n", 0, 0, 0, "same" },

    -- ── Tuple return type ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    -- `gguf.read(string)` in tools/wasm/src/nn/init.duo, and gap[046] §6's
    -- third-ranked win. Note the pair: the incumbent accepts it only under the
    -- `fun` keyword and REJECTS the bare declaration, so the second row is a
    -- zigrej. The self-hosted parser reads both, which is the GR-001 rule
    -- applied consistently — a bare declaration is one whose params or return
    -- carry a type, and a tuple is a type.
    { "fun g(d, p): (str, i64)\n  d\nend\n", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "g(d, p): (str, i64)\n  d\nend\n", 1, 0, 0, "-" },

    -- ── `: T | error` — Pass 100 §0.6, and F3 ───────────────────────────────
    -- gap[046] measured this at zero: no uses in lib/std, no production in
    -- either half. It is NORM rather than "same" because the incumbent LOSES
    -- it — `f(x: i64): i64 | error` formats to `fun f(x: i64) -> i64`, union
    -- gone. So proj(S) carries `(ret (union i64 error))` and proj(fmt(S))
    -- carries `(ret i64)`, and the pinned difference IS the finding.
    { "f(x: i64): i64 | error\n  x\nend\n", 0, 0, 0, "norm" },
    { "f(x: i64): str | error\n  x\nend\n", 0, 0, 0, "norm" },

    -- ── const · defer ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    -- gap[046] §6 ranked these last on cheapness and that was right. `defer` is
    -- a zigrej: the incumbent has no production for it at all, so adding one
    -- here makes the self-hosted parser strictly more capable. Pinned as the
    -- divergence it is rather than hidden.
    { "const k = 5", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "f(h: any): void\n  defer close(h)\nend\n", 1, 0, 0, "-" },

    -- ── Chained comparison ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    -- `a > b != c > d`, the boolean-XOR idiom in lib/std/math/geometry.duo and
    -- tools/wasm/src/engine.duo. The level read exactly one comparison and left
    -- the second operator for the statement level to reject.
    { "x = a > b != c > d", 0, 0, 0, "same" },

    -- ── String-literal descriptor key, and a pack of EXPRESSIONS ────────────
    -- Both from the last two non-deny-list `tools` rejections. The pack row is
    -- the sharper one: the comma list is read with the TARGET grammar on the
    -- speculation that an `=` follows, so a second element that is arithmetic
    -- (`s, pos + 8 + len`) died on the `+`.
    { "t = { \"a b\" = 1 }", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
    { "f(d: any): any\n  s = 1\n  s, d + 8\nend\n", 0, 0, 0, "same" },

    -- ── DECLARED NORMALIZATIONS ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    -- P3 fails on these and should: the incumbent desugars, so the two
    -- projections are of two different programs and both are correct. Pinned
    -- rather than skipped, so a change in what the incumbent desugars surfaces
    -- here instead of hiding as noise.
    --
    -- `local` is erased (deny list §1), so fmt(S) is a plain binding.
    { "local n: i64 = 5", 0, 0, 0, "norm" },
    -- Hex folds to decimal and redundant parens drop: same shape, different
    -- leaf text.
    { "v = a | ((b & 0x7f) << s)", 0, 0, 0, "norm" },
    -- The `nn { }` DSL: the incumbent has a dedicated production rewriting it
    -- to `req("std.ml.nn").build(...)`. gap[046] §3 called this parser's
    -- reading the first of two "accepted but WRONG" misprojections; the
    -- differential reclassifies it as a MISSING DESUGARING, which is a
    -- different repair and a much smaller one.
    { "m = nn { relu }", 0, 0, 0, "norm" },

    -- ── P1 zigrej: this parser is MORE permissive ───────────────────────────
    -- `fun NAME(params): T = expr end` is pinned as a POSITIVE in
    -- pass16_parser_corpus_proof.duo row 4, and the incumbent rejects it:
    -- "expected expression, got '='". The corpus proof pins a projection for a
    -- construct that is not in the incumbent's language. Recorded here as the
    -- finding it is — the repair is a decision about the grammar, not a patch
    -- to either parser.
    { "fun add(a: i64, b: i64): i64 = a + b end", 1, 0, 0, "-" },

    -- ── F2: the formatter is not idempotent ─────────────────────────────────
    -- `duo fmt` renders `f = fun(a) a end` as `f = (a) / a / end` and then
    -- CANNOT PARSE IT. Both oracles reject, so this is not a hole in the
    -- self-hosted parser — it is a src/pretty.zig round-trip defect. The
    -- single-name group `(a)` is genuinely undecidable without the enclosing
    -- statement's base column (see is_bare_lambda_head), and this row is why
    -- that was left open rather than guessed at: the shape does not occur in
    -- any real file, only in the formatter's own broken output.
    { "f = (a)\n  a\nend\n", 1, 1, 0, "-" },

    -- ── F1: CLOSED — the lens round-trips ───────────────────────────────────
    -- WAS (0, 0, 1, "norm"): `has(.err)(r)` formatted to
    -- `has(|__proj_v| __proj_v.err)(r)` — a pipe-delimited closure that is not
    -- Duo grammar, with a generated name breaking LAW-ONE twice over. 21 files,
    -- the largest remaining P2 class. src/pretty.zig now re-sugars the lens, so
    -- fmt(S) IS S: the Duo parser reads it (p2 = 0) and the projections are
    -- identical ("same"). Teaching THIS parser to read `|x| x.err` would have
    -- been annexing foreign surface for a printer bug, which A2 forbids.
    --
    -- THE PIN WAS THE GATE. With the printer repaired and this row unchanged,
    -- the harness returned 100 + 36 = 136 — the P2 mismatch for row 36, since
    -- the row still asserted the defect. 136 reads as 128+8 and was once
    -- mistaken for a signal; the harness has NO print statements, so zero
    -- stdout is what it produces at exit 0 as well and is not evidence of a
    -- crash. A row that encodes a defect must be re-pinned when the defect is
    -- repaired, or it convicts the repair.
    { "x = has(.err)(r)", 0, 0, 0, "same" },

    -- ── P1 duorej: holes that remain, pinned so they cannot regress silently ─
    -- Unlike `then` and `->`, the formatter does not EMIT these, so a program
    -- this parser accepts never formats into one. They stay rejections by
    -- choice, and the rows pin the choice so the next agent inherits a
    -- measurement instead of an argument.
    { "fun v(t: i64): str\n  match t\n  case 1 \"a\"\n  end\nend\n", 0, 1, 0, "-" },
    { "q = { k for k in xs }", 0, 1, 0, "-" },

    -- ── F4: the incumbent repairs a truncated block; this parser does not ────
    -- `do x = 1` parses CLEAN under src/parser.zig and formats to a closed
    -- `do / x = 1 / end`. Routing `do` through proj_body made this parser
    -- equally lenient, and pass16_parser_corpus_proof.duo was RED on it (exit
    -- 117) at HEAD before this session. `do` is an explicit block whose only
    -- terminator is `end` — unlike an offside body, where EOF closes because
    -- §0.7 deleted `end` — so proj_block requires it. Deliberate strictness,
    -- pinned as a duorej divergence rather than presented as agreement.
    { "do x = 1", 0, 1, 0, "-" },

    -- ── BOTH REJECT: the compile-fail differential ──────────────────────────
    -- Malformed input must be rejected by both, or the self-hosted parser is
    -- inventing structure.
    { "x =", 1, 1, 0, "-" },
    { "fun f(", 1, 1, 0, "-" },
    { "x = t[i", 1, 1, 0, "-" },

    -- ── F2: the lambda now ROUND-TRIPS, and "same" is the whole point ───────
    -- APPENDED, never inserted: the rows above are addressed by INDEX in the
    -- exit code, and row 36's comment cites 136 = 100 + 36 by number.
    --
    -- `f = fun(a) a + 1 end` used to format to a multi-line `f = (a) / a + 1 /
    -- end`, which does not reparse — `f = (a)` binds a parenthesized expression
    -- and the `end` is then orphaned ("expected '<eof>', got 'end'"). The
    -- lambda now keeps its `fun`, so fmt(S) is `f = fun(a) / a + 1 / end` and
    -- the projection is IDENTICAL to S's.
    --
    -- THE STABILITY FIELD IS LOAD-BEARING HERE. The obvious repair — render a
    -- sole-expression body as the one-line `(params) expr` — passes P2, because
    -- `f = (a) a + 1` PARSES. It parses as a different program:
    --
    --     f = fun(a) a + 1 end  ->  (assign f (lambda (params (param a)) (+ a 1)))
    --     f = (a) a + 1         ->  (assign f a) (+ a 1)        TWO statements
    --
    -- P2 alone would have waved that through and the formatter would have begun
    -- emitting a silent misparse — worse than the rejection it replaced, since
    -- `is_bare_lambda_head` is undecidable without the statement's base column.
    -- P3 is what caught it, at 200 + 43. Pinned "same", so any future repair
    -- that reintroduces the bare head is convicted here rather than shipped.
    { "f = fun(a) a + 1 end", 0, 0, 0, "same" },
}

main(): i64
    std.script.fs_mkdir(tmpdir())

    -- Positive control on the oracle itself, both directions. Without it, a
    -- missing or non-writing `duo fmt` makes every row read "zig accepts,
    -- output unchanged" and the whole harness reports a confident green.
    if zig_accepts("x = 1\n") != 0
        return 250
    end
    if zig_accepts("x =\n") == 0
        return 250
    end

    for i = 1, #rows
        r = rows[i]
        src = r[1]
        z = zig_accepts(src)
        d = duo_accepts(src)
        zbit: i64 = 1
        if z == 0
            zbit = 0
        end
        dbit: i64 = 1
        if d == 0
            dbit = 0
        end
        if zbit != r[2] or dbit != r[3]
            return i
        end
        -- P2/P3 only where BOTH oracles accepted the source. Where the Duo
        -- parser already rejects S, it rejects fmt(S) for the same reason —
        -- `match` survives formatting — and reporting that as a second,
        -- separate failure would double-count one finding and bury the case P2
        -- exists for: S accepted, fmt(S) NOT.
        if zbit == 0 and dbit == 0
            rendered = zig_render()
            p2: i64 = 1
            if duo_accepts(rendered) == 0
                p2 = 0
            end
            if p2 != r[4]
                return 100 + i
            end
            a = std.compiler.parser.proj_program(src, "probe.duo")
            b = std.compiler.parser.proj_program(rendered, "probe.duo")
            same = a == b
            if r[5] == "same" and not same
                return 200 + i
            end
            if r[5] == "norm" and same
                return 200 + i
            end
        end
    end
    0
end
